 TASK A3 – DESIGN WITH POINTERS (5 Marks)

 A3.1 – Pointer Operations (2 Marks)

Given:

```c
int stock[8];
int *p = stock;
```

* `p + 3` – Points to the 4th element.

* `*(p + 3)` – Value of the 4th element.

* `p[3]` – Same as `*(p + 3)`.

* `*p++` – Accesses the current element, then moves the pointer to the next element.

Memory diagram:

```text
Array index:  0    1    2    3    4    5    6    7
             [ ]  [ ]  [ ]  [ ]  [ ]  [ ]  [ ]  [ ]
              ↑              ↑
              p            p+3
```

### A3.2 – Pointers and Functions (2 Marks)

```c
int *findLowestStock(int stock[], int n)
{
    int *lowest = &stock[0];

    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++)
    {
        if (stock[i] < *lowest)
            lowest = &stock[i];
    }

    return lowest;
}
```

This function returns a pointer to the element with the lowest stock. The caller can update the value directly using `*ptr`.

Returning a pointer to a local variable is unsafe because the local variable is destroyed when the function ends, causing a dangling pointer.

